C = [
("h1", "3. The Whirlwind and What Came After"),
("p", "Claim: Elijah’s departure is the second fact the promise rests on. He did not die. He “went up by a whirlwind into heaven” (2 Kings 2:11), and the text of Kings shows at once that the prophets of the day treated the matter as open and that his spirit was seen on another man. Both facts bear on Malachi 4:5. The first says that the man named in the promise has not been reported dead. The second says that a man may bear Elijah’s spirit and not be Elijah, which is the point on which the Gospels’ account of John turns. A writing from Elijah is also recorded in Chronicles, and the volume reports the passage and builds nothing on its timing."),
("sub", "3.1 The last journey"),
("p", "The chapter opens with its own announcement: “when the LORD would take up Elijah into heaven by a whirlwind, that Elijah went with Elisha from Gilgal” (2 Kings 2:1). Three times Elijah tells Elisha to tarry, because the LORD has sent him to Beth-el, to Jericho and to Jordan, and three times Elisha answers, “As the LORD liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee” (2 Kings 2:2, 4, 6). At Beth-el and at Jericho the sons of the prophets say the same thing to Elisha: “Knowest thou that the LORD will take away thy master from thy head to day?” (2 Kings 2:3, 5). The event is known beforehand to the prophets’ company, and Elisha’s answer, “Yea, I know it; hold ye your peace,” shows that he knows it too."),
("p", "At Jordan fifty men stand “to view afar off,” and Elijah “took his mantle, and wrapped it together, and smote the waters, and they were divided hither and thither, so that they two went over on dry ground” (2 Kings 2:7, 8). The mantle that was cast on Elisha in 1 Kings 19:19 is the instrument here. The crossing is of the river Israel crossed under Joshua, on dry ground, and the text does not draw the parallel; the volume notes the geography and no more."),
("sub", "3.2 A double portion"),
("p", "On the farther bank Elijah says, “Ask what I shall do for thee, before I be taken away from thee,” and Elisha asks, “let a double portion of thy spirit be upon me” (2 Kings 2:9). The answer is carefully worded. “Thou hast asked a hard thing: nevertheless, if thou see me when I am taken from thee, it shall be so unto thee; but if not, it shall not be so” (2 Kings 2:10). The gift is not Elijah’s to give, and the sign that it is granted is that Elisha sees the departure."),
("p", "Then the event itself: “there appeared a chariot of fire, and horses of fire, and parted them both asunder; and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven” (2 Kings 2:11). Elisha cries, “My father, my father, the chariot of Israel, and the horsemen thereof. And he saw him no more” (2 Kings 2:12). The words “he saw him no more” are the narrator’s report of what Elisha saw, and the next verses show what the prophets’ company concluded."),
("sub", "3.3 The spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha"),
("p", "Elisha takes up the mantle that fell, returns to Jordan and asks, “Where is the LORD God of Elijah?” (2 Kings 2:13, 14). The waters part for him, and the sons of the prophets at Jericho, who had watched from the other side, say, “The spirit of Elijah doth rest on Elisha” (2 Kings 2:15). They bow to the ground before him. A man other than Elijah is recognized as bearing Elijah’s spirit, and the recognition is made by the prophets themselves. He is not called Elijah, and the narrator does not call him Elijah. He is Elisha, the son of Shaphat, “which poured water on the hands of Elijah” (2 Kings 3:11), and Jehoshaphat says of him, “The word of the LORD is with him” (2 Kings 3:12)."),
("p", "This is the precedent the volume wants. The Old Testament itself shows that the spirit of Elijah can rest on a man who is not Elijah, that the prophets can say so in those words, and that the man is received in that character. When Gabriel says that a son of Zacharias will go before the Lord “in the spirit and power of Elias” (Luke 1:17), the form of the sentence is the form of 2 Kings 2:15. The sentence does not say that the man will be Elijah, and the volume reads it so in chapter 6."),
("sub", "3.4 The search of three days"),
("p", "The sons of the prophets are not content. “Behold now, there be with thy servants fifty strong men; let them go, we pray thee, and seek thy master: lest peradventure the Spirit of the LORD hath taken him up, and cast him upon some mountain, or into some valley” (2 Kings 2:16). Elisha forbids it, then yields when they urge him “till he was ashamed,” and they “sought three days, but found him not” (2 Kings 2:17). When they return, he says only, “Did I not say unto you, Go not?” (2 Kings 2:18)."),
("p", "The passage gives the company’s fear and the failure of the search, and it gives nothing further. It does not say where Elijah is. It says he went up “into heaven,” and it says that three days of searching by fifty men found no body. The volume draws from this a narrow point. Kings reports Elijah’s going and does not report his death, and no passage of the Old Testament says he died. Hebrews 11:5 says the same of Enoch, “was not found, because God had translated him,” and the volume cites that line only to note that Scripture records two men removed without death and does not explain how."),
("sub", "3.5 The writing to Jehoram"),
("p", "One more text in the Old Testament names Elijah after the whirlwind, and it is a hard one. In the days of Jehoram of Judah, “there came a writing to him from Elijah the prophet, saying, Thus saith the LORD God of David thy father, Because thou hast not walked in the ways of Jehoshaphat thy father, nor in the ways of Asa king of Judah, but hast walked in the way of the kings of Israel, and hast made Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem to go a whoring, like to the whoredoms of the house of Ahab, and also hast slain thy brethren of thy father’s house, which were better than thyself: behold, with a great plague will the LORD smite thy people, and thy children, and thy wives, and all thy goods: and thou shalt have great sickness by disease of thy bowels” (2 Chronicles 21:12–15)."),
("p", "The passage gives a writing from “Elijah the prophet” and gives no date for the writing in relation to the whirlwind. The chronological notices that join the reigns of Judah and Israel in Kings and Chronicles are a known difficulty, and readers differ on whether the writing was sent before the whirlwind or after it. The volume builds nothing on the point. It reports that the writing is in the record, that it comes in the name of “the LORD God of David thy father,” and that its subject is Judah’s king and not Israel’s. The prophet’s word reaches the house of David, and it is a word of judgment on a king who followed Ahab’s house."),
("ctbl", "Elijah after the Kings account, as the passages state it.", [
    ("Passage", "What is stated", "What is not stated"),
    ("2 Kings 2:1, 11", "Taken up by a whirlwind into heaven; a chariot and horses of fire", "That he died; where he is"),
    ("2 Kings 2:9, 10", "A double portion of his spirit asked; granted if Elisha sees him go", "That the gift is Elijah’s to give"),
    ("2 Kings 2:15", "“The spirit of Elijah doth rest on Elisha”", "That Elisha is called Elijah"),
    ("2 Kings 2:16–18", "Fifty men search three days and find nothing", "Any report of his body"),
    ("2 Kings 3:11, 12", "Elisha, who poured water on Elijah’s hands; “The word of the LORD is with him”", "Any further word of Elijah in that chapter"),
    ("2 Chronicles 21:12–15", "A writing from Elijah the prophet to Jehoram of Judah", "When the writing was sent"),
  ], [0.26, 0.4, 0.34]),
("sub", "3.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Elijah was taken up by a whirlwind into heaven, and the Old Testament reports no death.",
    "The sons of the prophets treated his going as open: a search of three days, and no report of a body.",
    "The prophets said of Elisha, “The spirit of Elijah doth rest on Elisha,” and he is received so without being called Elijah. A man may bear Elijah’s spirit and not be Elijah.",
    "A writing from Elijah to Jehoram of Judah is in the record; the volume reports it and builds nothing on its timing.",
    "Malachi’s promise, “I will send you Elijah the prophet,” is made after all of this, and names the man with the title he bore in Kings.",
  ]),
]
